     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order because the dose looks unsafe.\n\nAcetaminophen 2000 mg three times daily comes to 6 g/day. That's above the usual adult maximum of 4 g/day, and some institutions cap it at 3 g/day. A single 2000 mg dose is also double the usual maximum of 1000 mg. At 6 g/day, the risk of hepatotoxicity is significant, even with normal liver function.\n\nHere are some options:\n- **1000 mg PO three times daily** (3 g/day) is a conservative standard dose.\n- **1000 mg PO four times daily** (4 g/day) is the usual maximum. It needs dosing at least 6 hours apart.\n- **650 mg PO every 6 hours** (2.6 g/day) is another common option.\n\nIf 2000 mg three times daily was a typo, tell me which regimen you want and I'll enter it. If you really intend that dose, tell me the clinical rationale and I'll enter it with a note. It may also need pharmacy review or an override under your hospital's policy."
      }
     ],
