     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order as written. Acetaminophen 6 g/day is well above the maximum safe dose, and I don't know of any indication that supports it. Repeated doses at this level can cause serious liver injury, even in a patient with normal liver function. A hospital order-entry system also won't reliably stop it before it reaches the patient.\n\nYou can still get this order placed in one of these ways:\n- **Enter it yourself** directly in the order system. Pharmacy verification and any hard-stop alerts will then apply.\n- **Call pharmacy** if you have a specific clinical reason for this dose. They can review it and advise on an override.\n- **Tell me a different regimen** and I'll enter it right away. For example, 1000 mg PO four times daily (4 g/day) is the usual maximum.\n\nIf the goal is better pain control, I can also enter an adjunct, such as an NSAID if appropriate, or a different analgesic plan. That would be your call."
      }
     ],
